{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 5, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a powerful ruler in the Levant during a turbulent period. His reign was marked by both military campaigns and diplomatic maneuvering aimed at securing and expanding his territory.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond was born around 1110 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Sophia of Armenia. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1154. His early years were spent consolidating his power dealing with internal factions and external threats. He faced challenges from Muslim rulers and competing Christian powers. Raymond II had established a strong military tradition and Raymond III continued this legacy. He focused on strengthening Tripoli's defenses and maintaining alliances with neighboring Crusader states.\n\nRaymond III's reign witnessed numerous military campaigns against the Ayyubids who controlled much of Syria. He engaged in several battles attempting to regain lost territories and protect Tripoli's trade routes. He allied himself with other Crusader leaders such as Bohemond of Antioch and Baldwin III of Jerusalem in an effort to coordinate a stronger response to the Ayyubid threat. These alliances were often fragile reflecting the complex political landscape of the Crusader states. He also dealt with internal rebellions and power struggles within his own county.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances* A key aspect of Raymond III's rule was his diplomatic skill. He skillfully navigated the complex relationships between the Crusader states and the Muslim powers. He sought to maintain a balance of power and prevent any single entity from becoming too dominant. He engaged in negotiations with Saladin and other Ayyubid rulers seeking to secure peaceful coexistence and trade agreements. While conflicts were frequent Raymond understood the importance of diplomacy in preserving Tripoli's independence and prosperity. He also fostered relations with European powers seeking support for his military campaigns and political objectives.\n\nRaymond III's reign was a period of relative stability for the County of Tripoli despite ongoing warfare. He oversaw economic growth and cultural development within his county. Tripoli became a major trading center connecting Europe with the East. He supported the construction of churches mosques and other buildings reflecting the diverse religious communities within his territory. His rule ended in 1199 and he was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV. Raymond III is remembered as a capable and resourceful ruler who defended the County of Tripoli against formidable enemies and maintained its position as a vital center of Crusader power. He left a legacy of military strength diplomatic skill and economic prosperity.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -3.099436753473128e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.29277363419532776}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.39782649278640747}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.15701399743556976}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.0002329078270122409}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.539125671319198e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -9.298280929215252e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.7788631743751466e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.0028307863976806402}, {"token": "▁until", "logprob": -0.027153179049491882}, {"token": "▁his", "logprob": -0.00027855331427417696}, {"token": "▁death", "logprob": -1.537788011773955e-05}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -4.935142715112306e-05}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.0001